# No `var` in new C# code

New or modified C# code must declare variables with their explicit type. The **only** allowed use of `var` is a LINQ projection whose element type is anonymous, because the compiler requires it there.

This supersedes the softer guideline in `loadly-aggregator-architecture` → "C# style".

## Hard rules

- ❌ Do NOT use `var` in local variable declarations in new code — this includes `new T()` right-hand sides, factory and async calls, `foreach`, `using`, tuple deconstruction, `out var`, and pattern-matching declarations.
- ✅ Allowed exception: LINQ projection to an anonymous type.

  ```csharp
  var summary = contracts
      .Select(c => new { c.Id, c.Name, Total = c.Trips.Count })
      .ToList();
  ```

  The moment the projection returns a nameable type, use that type on the LHS.

## Examples

```csharp
// ❌ bad
var admin = await _adminRepository.GetByIdAsync(id);
var list = new List<AdminDto>();
foreach (var trip in trips) { ... }
if (int.TryParse(raw, out var value)) { ... }
var (a, b) = tuple;
await using var scope = _scopeFactory.Create();

// ✅ good
Admin? admin = await _adminRepository.GetByIdAsync(id);
List<AdminDto> list = new();                 // target-typed `new` is fine — LHS is explicit
foreach (Trip trip in trips) { ... }
if (int.TryParse(raw, out int value)) { ... }
(int a, string b) = tuple;
await using IServiceScope scope = _scopeFactory.Create();
```

## Why

Explicit types make reviews and diffs self-explanatory, prevent silent return-type changes from rippling through callers, and keep our Clean Architecture layer boundaries legible without jumping to definitions.

## Scope

Applies to any new or modified `.cs` file across API, Domain, Infrastructure, Shared, and Tests. Pre-existing `var` in untouched code is grandfathered — do not do drive-by rewrites. Only rewrite `var` on lines you are already changing for another reason.
